────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  COMPLEXITY TIER (issue #159)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Iteration 1 declared tier 1: single mechanism, single knob, treatment vs control
  Justification: iter 1, single mechanism (preemption metric accounting), treatment vs control
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  We are testing whether preempted requests cause double-counting of output tokens in BLIS's metrics. When KV cache is constrained (600 blocks), requests get preempted and restarted, but their previously-generated decode tokens are counted again — inflating total_output_tokens by ~9% (114,630 vs 105,082). The fix stores the decoded token count before resetting progress on preemption, then subtracts it from the running total when the request is rescheduled.

  * Two experiment arms: h-main runs the same 200-request workload (qwen3-14b, rate=20, seed-controlled) with small cache (600 KV blocks, causes preemptions) and large cache (60,000 KV blocks, no preemptions) — after the fix, total_output_tokens should be identical in both cases.
  * h-control-negative verifies no regression: with the large cache (no preemptions), the fix should produce exactly the same total_output_tokens (105,082) as the unpatched code, confirming the fix is inert when no preemptions occur.
  * Code changes touch three files: batch_formation.go (capture decoded token count before ProgressIndex reset), simulator.go (subtract captured count from TotalOutputTokens during preemption processing), and simulator_test.go (regression test asserting equal counts with/without preemptions).
  * Confirmation criterion: total_output_tokens matches between small-cache and large-cache runs post-fix. Refutation: if they still differ, diagnostic steps include checking DecodedTokens computation, alternative TotalOutputTokens increment paths, and force-completion edge cases.
  * Design is minimal and appropriate for a Tier-1 binary bug fix — no ablation or dose-response arms are needed since the mechanism is present/absent with no tunable parameters.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

